From: Fabien Danieau <fabien.danieau@pollen-robotics.com>
Subject: [PATCH] rk3566-radxa-zero-3w-aic8800ds2: enable the Radxa Camera 8M (IMX219)

The board could not see a camera at all, and plugging one in would not have
changed that: rk3566-radxa-zero3.dtsi describes no camera, so i2c2, csi2_dphy0,
csi2_dphy_hw, rkcif and rkisp all stay disabled. Only the CSI2 host controller
probes, with nothing attached to it, and there is no /dev/video*. The sensor's
i2c address is never even scanned, so an empty connector and a populated one
look identical from software.

The drivers were never the problem -- CONFIG_VIDEO_IMX219, CONFIG_VIDEO_OV5647,
CONFIG_VIDEO_IMX415, CONFIG_VIDEO_GC2093, CONFIG_VIDEO_ROCKCHIP_ISP and
CONFIG_VIDEO_ROCKCHIP_CIF are all =y already. This is purely the missing
description.

Transcribed from radxa's radxa-zero3-rpi-camera-v2.dts overlay rather than
written from scratch: the Radxa Camera 8M is an IMX219 module electrically
equivalent to the Raspberry Pi Camera v2, so radxa's description applies
unchanged, and it is the only version of this path known to work on this board.

Compiled in rather than shipped as an overlay because the image boots one fixed
DTB through extlinux and every microduck carries this camera.

Pin conflicts were checked rather than assumed, since two other buses on this
board are already hand-assigned: i2c2m1 is GPIO4_B4/B5, the motor bus holds
UART2m0 on GPIO0_D0/D1 (see 0002) and bluetooth holds UART1m0 on GPIO2_B3/B4.
No overlap, and gpio3 RK_PC6 has no other user in the zero3 dtsi.

Applies with or without 0002, which edits a different part of the file.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [configuration]

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3566-radxa-zero-3w-aic8800ds2.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3566-radxa-zero-3w-aic8800ds2.dts
@@ -139,4 +139,155 @@
 			rockchip,pins = <3 RK_PD3 RK_FUNC_GPIO &pcfg_pull_down>;
 		};
 	};
-};
\ No newline at end of file
+};
+
+/*
+ * Radxa Camera 8M (IMX219) on the 15-pin CSI connector.
+ *
+ * Transcribed from radxa's own radxa-zero3-rpi-camera-v2.dts overlay, which is
+ * the only proven description of this path for this board. The Camera 8M is an
+ * IMX219 module electrically equivalent to the Raspberry Pi Camera v2, so the
+ * overlay applies unchanged.
+ *
+ * It is compiled in rather than shipped as an overlay because the image boots
+ * one fixed DTB through extlinux and every microduck carries this camera; an
+ * overlay would only add a step that can be forgotten.
+ *
+ * Nothing here is a free choice -- the sensor's i2c address, the 24 MHz xvclk,
+ * the two data lanes and the dphy0 -> rkisp routing are all fixed by the
+ * hardware. The only thing worth knowing when reading it is that the whole
+ * chain must be enabled together: the sensor is useless without csi2_dphy0,
+ * and rkisp will not finish binding its async subdevs (so no /dev/video*
+ * appears at all) unless every endpoint it declares resolves.
+ *
+ * Pins were checked against the other two buses this board already claims:
+ * i2c2m1 is GPIO4_B4/B5, while the motor bus holds UART2m0 on GPIO0_D0/D1 and
+ * bluetooth holds UART1m0 on GPIO2_B3/B4. No overlap, and gpio3 RK_PC6 is
+ * otherwise unused in the zero3 dtsi.
+ */
+
+/ {
+	clk_cam_24m: external-camera-clock-24m {
+		compatible = "fixed-clock";
+		clock-frequency = <24000000>;
+		clock-output-names = "clk_cam_24m";
+		#clock-cells = <0>;
+		status = "okay";
+	};
+
+	/*
+	 * Not referenced by the sensor node: regulator-always-on/boot-on is
+	 * radxa's way of simply asserting the module's enable line at boot and
+	 * leaving it asserted. Kept as-is rather than converted to a
+	 * pwdn-gpios on the sensor, which is what the OV5647 overlay does --
+	 * the imx219 driver in this tree does not take one.
+	 */
+	camera_pwdn_gpio: camera-pwdn-gpio {
+		compatible = "regulator-fixed";
+		regulator-name = "camera_pwdn_gpio";
+		regulator-always-on;
+		regulator-boot-on;
+		enable-active-high;
+		gpio = <&gpio3 RK_PC6 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+		status = "okay";
+	};
+};
+
+&i2c2 {
+	status = "okay";
+	pinctrl-names = "default";
+	pinctrl-0 = <&i2c2m1_xfer>;
+	#address-cells = <1>;
+	#size-cells = <0>;
+
+	camera_imx219: camera-imx219@10 {
+		compatible = "sony,imx219";
+		reg = <0x10>;
+		clocks = <&clk_cam_24m>;
+		clock-names = "xvclk";
+		rockchip,camera-module-index = <0>;
+		rockchip,camera-module-facing = "back";
+		/*
+		 * A lookup key into rockchip's camera tuning data, not a
+		 * description of the part. Left at radxa's value so that any
+		 * IQ file that does exist is still found; the Camera 8M is the
+		 * same module under a different label.
+		 */
+		rockchip,camera-module-name = "rpi-camera-v2";
+		rockchip,camera-module-lens-name = "default";
+		status = "okay";
+
+		port {
+			ucam_out0: endpoint {
+				remote-endpoint = <&mipi_in_ucam0>;
+				data-lanes = <1 2>;
+			};
+		};
+	};
+};
+
+&csi2_dphy_hw {
+	status = "okay";
+};
+
+&csi2_dphy0 {
+	status = "okay";
+
+	ports {
+		#address-cells = <1>;
+		#size-cells = <0>;
+
+		port@0 {
+			reg = <0>;
+			#address-cells = <1>;
+			#size-cells = <0>;
+
+			mipi_in_ucam0: endpoint@1 {
+				reg = <1>;
+				remote-endpoint = <&ucam_out0>;
+				data-lanes = <1 2>;
+			};
+		};
+
+		port@1 {
+			reg = <1>;
+			#address-cells = <1>;
+			#size-cells = <0>;
+
+			dphy0_out: endpoint@1 {
+				reg = <1>;
+				remote-endpoint = <&isp0_in>;
+			};
+		};
+	};
+};
+
+&rkisp_vir0 {
+	status = "okay";
+
+	port {
+		#address-cells = <1>;
+		#size-cells = <0>;
+
+		isp0_in: endpoint@0 {
+			reg = <0>;
+			remote-endpoint = <&dphy0_out>;
+		};
+	};
+};
+
+&rkisp {
+	status = "okay";
+};
+
+&rkisp_mmu {
+	status = "okay";
+};
+
+&rkcif_mmu {
+	status = "okay";
+};
+
+&rkcif {
+	status = "okay";
+};
